1. Problem Statement

Enterprise applications do not fail or degrade in isolation. Their behavior is shaped by:
	•	infrastructure utilization
	•	log and error patterns
	•	incidents raised by operations
	•	upstream traffic behavior
	•	downstream dependency health
	•	resilience controls implemented in code
	•	actual resilience behavior under fault conditions

Today, these are usually observed in separate tools:
	•	Prometheus for metrics
	•	Splunk for logs
	•	ServiceNow for incidents
	•	Git for code scanning
	•	Net Havoc for resilience testing

Because these signals are fragmented, teams face major problems:

1.1 Incomplete capacity decisions

A service may appear over-utilized, but the actual reason may be:
	•	heavy upstream traffic,
	•	repeated retries from callers,
	•	slow downstream systems,
	•	poor timeout or circuit-breaker handling.

So looking only at CPU and memory gives wrong conclusions.

1.2 Incomplete resilience understanding

A service may have resilience code patterns in Git, but:
	•	the configuration may be weak,
	•	the controls may not be applied on critical paths,
	•	the service may still fail during real disruptions.

So code presence alone is not enough.

1.3 Hidden service-chain impact

Many incidents originate in one service but surface in another.
For example:
	•	a downstream DB slows down,
	•	the middle service retries excessively,
	•	an upstream service times out,
	•	incidents get raised against the wrong app.

Without upstream/downstream awareness, the wrong team gets blamed and the wrong action is taken.

1.4 Lack of actionable operating intelligence

Raw dashboards and point tools provide data, but not coordinated, evidence-backed decisions.
Engineering teams need a system that can:
	•	gather context,
	•	reason over dependencies,
	•	choose the correct operating playbook,
	•	execute the playbook,
	•	and publish trustworthy findings.

Final Problem Statement

Build a production-grade agent platform that uses playbooks to analyze application capacity, incidents, resilience posture, and upstream/downstream dependencies, then produce evidence-backed engineering findings and recommendations per application and per service chain.

