2. Solution Definition

2.1 What you are building

You are building a playbook-driven engineering agent platform.

This is not just a dashboard.
This is not just a rules engine.
This is not just a report generator.

It is an agentic decision system.

The system should:
	•	collect signals from all source systems
	•	assemble a unified application context
	•	understand upstream and downstream dependencies
	•	choose the appropriate playbook
	•	execute that playbook step by step
	•	optionally trigger resilience validation
	•	produce findings, scores, evidence, and recommendations

⸻

2.2 What the agent operates on

The agent must operate on application context, not raw source data.

A proper application context should include:
	•	application identity
	•	service ownership
	•	environment
	•	Prometheus utilization history
	•	Splunk log/error patterns
	•	ServiceNow incident patterns
	•	Git-scanned resilience attributes
	•	Net Havoc validation results
	•	upstream services
	•	downstream services
	•	dependency risk indicators
	•	freshness/confidence of data

This context is the input to playbook execution.

⸻

2.3 What a playbook means in this project

In your project, a playbook is an executable operating workflow for the agent.

A real playbook should define:
	•	when it should trigger
	•	what inputs it needs
	•	what checks it performs
	•	how it reasons
	•	what evidence it must collect
	•	what outputs it must produce
	•	whether it triggers additional workflows

So a playbook is not just:

if cpu > 80 then mark overutilized

It is more like:

for this application, gather last 30 days utilization, correlate incidents, inspect dependency pressure, determine whether the issue is local or propagated, assess resilience posture, decide whether a resilience validation run is needed, and publish an evidence-backed finding.

That is the correct model.

⸻

2.4 Major functional capabilities

A. Capacity intelligence

The platform should determine:
	•	under-utilized apps
	•	over-utilized apps
	•	unstable apps
	•	apps with incident-linked capacity risk
	•	apps affected by dependency-induced stress

B. Resilience intelligence

The platform should determine:
	•	whether resilience patterns exist in code
	•	what attributes are implemented
	•	whether those attributes are sufficient
	•	whether resilience was validated dynamically
	•	where resilience gaps exist

C. Dependency intelligence

The platform should determine:
	•	which upstream services influence load patterns
	•	which downstream services influence latency/errors
	•	whether an app’s issue is local or dependency-driven
	•	which service chains are most operationally fragile

D. Reporting intelligence

The platform should produce:
	•	app-level findings
	•	dependency-aware findings
	•	risk summaries
	•	evidence-backed recommendations
	•	audit-friendly playbook outputs

⸻

2.5 Production operating model

The flow should be:
	1.	collect data
	2.	normalize and map it to the right application
	3.	assemble application and dependency context
	4.	evaluate triggers
	5.	select playbooks
	6.	execute playbooks
	7.	store findings and evidence
	8.	publish reports and APIs

That is the production model.

