3. Detailed Roadmap

Now I’ll give you the actual roadmap in the correct sequence.

⸻

Phase 1: Define the Operating Model

Goal

Lock how the agent behaves before building connectors or logic.

What to do

Define clearly:
	•	what counts as an application
	•	what counts as a service
	•	how upstream and downstream are represented
	•	what the agent is allowed to do
	•	what a playbook can do
	•	what outputs are mandatory
	•	who the audience is for findings

You must finalize the playbook contract here.

Each playbook should have:
	•	name
	•	purpose
	•	trigger conditions
	•	required inputs
	•	optional inputs
	•	execution steps
	•	decision rules
	•	evidence requirements
	•	output schema
	•	severity model
	•	audit metadata

Also define whether the agent is:
	•	advisory only,
	•	advisory + test-triggering,
	•	or later action-triggering under controls.

Deliverables
	•	problem statement approved
	•	scope approved
	•	playbook contract approved
	•	output contract approved
	•	governance boundaries approved

Why this phase matters

If this is not defined first, the system becomes an uncontrolled set of scripts.

⸻

Phase 2: Build the Application Registry

Goal

Create a trusted identity model for every application and dependency.

What to do

Create a registry that maps:
	•	app name
	•	service name
	•	owning team
	•	environment
	•	repo URL
	•	Prometheus identifiers
	•	Splunk identifiers
	•	ServiceNow mapping
	•	Net Havoc target mapping
	•	upstream services
	•	downstream services

This registry is critical because all correlation depends on correct mapping.

You also need a method to mark:
	•	mapping confidence
	•	missing mappings
	•	exceptions
	•	manually curated overrides

Deliverables
	•	canonical application registry
	•	service/dependency registry
	•	source-system mapping model
	•	confidence and override mechanism

Why this phase matters

Without a registry, the agent cannot reason reliably across tools.

⸻

Phase 3: Build Source Connectors

Goal

Establish reliable data ingestion from all systems.

What to do

Build production-grade connectors for:
	•	Prometheus
	•	Splunk
	•	ServiceNow
	•	Git
	•	Net Havoc
	•	dependency metadata source if available

Connectors should support:
	•	retries
	•	pagination
	•	authentication handling
	•	error handling
	•	idempotent execution
	•	logging
	•	observability

Do not put business rules inside connectors.
Connectors should only fetch and hand off.

Deliverables
	•	working source integrations
	•	raw payload ingestion
	•	scheduling support
	•	connector monitoring

Why this phase matters

If source access is unstable, everything above it becomes unreliable.

⸻

Phase 4: Build Normalization Layer

Goal

Turn raw data into trusted internal entities.

What to do

Normalize raw source data into standard platform entities, such as:
	•	application
	•	dependency
	•	metric window
	•	incident
	•	log/error summary
	•	resilience attribute
	•	resilience validation result
	•	playbook run
	•	finding evidence

Define rules for:
	•	time-window alignment
	•	naming normalization
	•	duplicate removal
	•	incident filtering
	•	stale data flags
	•	confidence scoring

Deliverables
	•	normalized schemas
	•	common data contracts
	•	deduplication rules
	•	evidence objects

Why this phase matters

The agent cannot work directly on inconsistent source formats.

⸻

Phase 5: Build Topology and Dependency Layer

Goal

Make upstream/downstream analysis real.

What to do

Create a topology model that supports:
	•	direct upstream dependencies
	•	direct downstream dependencies
	•	multi-hop dependency chains
	•	dependency type classification
	•	dependency criticality
	•	confidence of topology discovery

Topology should include things like:
	•	caller services
	•	APIs
	•	databases
	•	queues
	•	caches
	•	batch dependencies if relevant

The system should support identifying:
	•	load propagation
	•	latency propagation
	•	failure propagation
	•	retry amplification
	•	incident propagation

Deliverables
	•	application dependency graph
	•	upstream/downstream traversal
	•	topology confidence model
	•	dependency impact model

Why this phase matters

This is what prevents wrong root-cause classification.

⸻

Phase 6: Build Context Assembly Layer

Goal

Create one decision-ready context per application.

What to do

Build a service that assembles all relevant information into a unified object.

For each app, context should include:
	•	application metadata
	•	recent utilization profile
	•	recent log/error profile
	•	incident summary
	•	upstream dependency state
	•	downstream dependency state
	•	resilience posture from Git
	•	resilience validation history
	•	missing data indicators
	•	freshness indicators
	•	confidence indicators

Also create service-chain context, not just app context, so the agent can reason across multi-service paths.

Deliverables
	•	app context model
	•	service-chain context model
	•	context assembly service
	•	freshness/confidence flags

Why this phase matters

The agent should operate on context, not on five raw systems.

⸻

Phase 7: Build the Playbook Engine

Goal

Create the actual execution mechanism of the agent.

What to do

Build a playbook engine that supports:
	•	playbook registration
	•	trigger evaluation
	•	playbook selection
	•	step execution
	•	evidence capture
	•	output generation
	•	execution history
	•	audit logging

The engine must support playbooks in categories such as:
	•	capacity playbooks
	•	incident-correlation playbooks
	•	dependency-impact playbooks
	•	resilience posture playbooks
	•	resilience validation playbooks
	•	reporting playbooks

Deliverables
	•	playbook runtime
	•	execution framework
	•	evidence collector
	•	audit trail
	•	playbook versioning model

Why this phase matters

This is the heart of the agent.

⸻

Phase 8: Build Capacity Playbooks

Goal

Make the capacity domain work end to end.

What to do

Create playbooks for:
	•	baseline utilization assessment
	•	under-utilization detection
	•	over-utilization detection
	•	incident-aware capacity assessment
	•	upstream-driven load analysis
	•	downstream-driven saturation analysis
	•	repeated stress pattern identification

The agent should be able to decide:
	•	whether the app is wasteful
	•	whether the app is stressed
	•	whether the stress is local
	•	whether the stress is dependency-induced
	•	whether incidents validate the concern

Deliverables
	•	first production capacity playbooks
	•	app-level capacity findings
	•	dependency-aware capacity findings

Why this phase matters

This creates the first real business value.

⸻

Phase 9: Build Resilience Posture Playbooks

Goal

Make static resilience analysis work end to end.

What to do

Create Git/code-based playbooks that identify:
	•	retry logic
	•	timeout controls
	•	circuit breakers
	•	fallback mechanisms
	•	bulkhead controls
	•	relevant configuration attributes

These playbooks should determine:
	•	whether resilience exists
	•	where it exists
	•	how complete it is
	•	what is missing on critical paths

Deliverables
	•	resilience posture playbooks
	•	app-level posture findings
	•	dependency-path posture findings

Why this phase matters

This reveals what the code claims to protect.

⸻

Phase 10: Build Resilience Validation Playbooks

Goal

Validate resilience behavior dynamically.

What to do

Create playbooks that determine:
	•	when to trigger Net Havoc tests
	•	what kind of fault to inject
	•	which dependencies to target
	•	how to observe recovery
	•	how to classify outcome

These playbooks should validate whether:
	•	timeout works,
	•	retry causes amplification,
	•	circuit breaking protects upstreams,
	•	recovery behavior is acceptable,
	•	service-chain resilience is adequate.

Deliverables
	•	Net Havoc execution playbooks
	•	resilience validation findings
	•	recovery/impact evidence

Why this phase matters

This validates real behavior, not just code presence.

⸻

Phase 11: Build Cross-Domain Decision Playbooks

Goal

Combine capacity, incidents, dependencies, and resilience.

What to do

Create playbooks that reason across all dimensions.

Examples:
	•	over-utilized app with repeated incident history and weak timeout controls
	•	stable app locally but stressed by upstream burst patterns
	•	downstream API causing latency propagation across multiple services
	•	under-utilized app with strong resilience posture but poor dependency chain health

These playbooks should create the highest-value conclusions.

Deliverables
	•	cross-domain playbooks
	•	risk-ranked findings
	•	service-chain recommendations

Why this phase matters

This turns separate analyses into true engineering intelligence.

⸻

Phase 12: Build Reporting and Publication Layer

Goal

Publish evidence-backed outputs for different audiences.

What to do

Create reporting outputs for:
	•	engineering teams
	•	service owners
	•	platform teams
	•	management

Reports should include:
	•	app summary
	•	dependency summary
	•	utilization conclusion
	•	resilience conclusion
	•	incident correlation
	•	service-chain impact
	•	evidence references
	•	recommendations
	•	severity/risk level

Expose through:
	•	APIs first
	•	dashboard later if needed

Deliverables
	•	reporting API
	•	structured report model
	•	evidence-linked findings

Why this phase matters

Without a strong output model, the platform won’t be adopted.

⸻

Phase 13: Governance, Controls, and Hardening

Goal

Make the platform production-safe.

What to do

Add:
	•	role-based access
	•	secret management
	•	execution approvals for risky playbooks
	•	full audit history
	•	retries and failure recovery
	•	health monitoring of the platform itself
	•	playbook versioning
	•	rollback capability
	•	observability dashboards for the agent

Also define:
	•	operational support model
	•	runbooks for connector failures
	•	incident handling for the platform itself

Deliverables
	•	hardened production platform
	•	supportability model
	•	operational controls
	•	audit and compliance readiness

Why this phase matters

A real enterprise agent must be governed, not just built.

